course: dictionary
lesson: 14048
title: Maracas
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A maraca is a musical instrument. It is a hollow ball or gourd with a handle, filled with seeds, beads or small stones. «She shook the maracas to keep the beat.» When you shake it, it makes a rattling sound, like rain on a tin roof.
explain: Context helps you here. The word «shake» tells you how you play it, and «beat» tells you it helps keep time in music. So a maraca is something you shake to make rhythm.
explain: Maracas are usually played in pairs, one in each hand. «The band played with a pair of maracas.» The singular is maraca, and the plural is maracas.
fact: A maraca is a percussion instrument: you shake it to make a sound.
fact: «Maraca» is a noun, and the plural is «maracas».
fact: Maracas are often used in Latin American music, from places like Cuba, Puerto Rico, Mexico and Venezuela.
fact: «A pair of maracas» means two maracas, usually one in each hand.
fact: You can make your own maracas in class from a plastic bottle or an egg filled with rice.
exercise: «She shook the maracas to keep the beat.» What do you do to a maraca to make it sound? | *Shake it | Pluck its strings | Strike it with a stick | Blow into it
exercise: What is a maraca usually filled with? | *Seeds, beads or small stones | Feathers and paper | Water and sand | Paint and glue
exercise: «The band played with a pair of maracas.» How many maracas make a pair? | One | *Two | Four | Three
test: Which phrase means two maracas together? | A beat of music | *A pair of maracas | A band of drums | A maraca handle
test: In which kind of music are maracas often used to keep the beat? | Silent films | Music with no rhythm | Music played only with a piano | *Latin American music
exam: Which sentence uses «maracas» correctly? | We ate maracas for lunch. | The maracas is a loud drum. | Maracas are a kind of tall tree. | *She shook a pair of maracas to keep the beat.
